ce:section.ce:para:
In our synthesis, the compound 9 crystallized as the ethanol solvate, once again demonstrating the stability of these copper(I) oxalate complexes towards protic solvents in the absence of air. In the crystal structure, the molecule is located on an inversion center, and the Cu(C2O4)Cu core is essentially planar, resulting in Ci
                            symmetry for the molecule (Fig. 9
                           ). Two of the CyNC ligands (related to one another by inversion symmetry) in each molecule have the isocyanide group in the axial position, while this group is in the equatorial position in the other two CyNC ligands (also related by inversion symmetry), accounting for the two NC bands seen in the infrared spectrum of this compound. The NC bond lengths in this complex are 1.150(3) and 1.156(3)Å, within the range of bond lengths for free isocyanides in the CSD (1.13–1.16Å for a wide variety of alkyl isocyanides), and the NC–Cu angles are close to 180° in both cases, further supporting a predominantly σ-bonding interaction between the isocyanide ligands and the copper centers.
